      "Aljafarawi was shot dead on 12 October 2025 in the Sabra neighbourhood of Gaza City. He is reported to have been hit by at least seven bullets. Footage that circulated afterwards is said to show his body wearing a press vest. The killing came during a fragile ceasefire, days before a summit in Egypt on the future of Gaza.",
      "WHAT IS SAID ABOUT WHO KILLED HIM:\nSeveral reports, including Arab News, said he was shot during clashes in Sabra between local security forces and armed members of the Doghmush clan. No court has established who fired the shots.\nThe Gaza Government Media Office condemned the killing as a direct result of Israel’s policy of targeting journalists through airstrikes and proxy militias.\nStatements by Gaza’s authorities described the attackers as collaborator gangs.\nPalestinian news reports listed in the sources said Israel-backed armed groups targeted him while he was documenting the damage from airstrikes.\nLocal authorities had not confirmed the details of the clash. Gaza’s Interior Ministry was reported to have opened an investigation.",
      "These accounts conflict and none has been independently verified.",
      "III. The claims made about him",
      "Before his death, Aljafarawi was the subject of public allegations. In September 2025 the Arabic-language spokesperson of the Israeli military, Avichay Adraee, alleged that he was affiliated with an armed group. His followers raised the alarm at the time, fearing that such an allegation could make him a target. Israeli media described him as linked to an armed group, and Israeli commentators criticised a video in which he appeared to praise the attacks of 7 October 2023.",
      "Aljafarawi denied any tie to an armed group and said he had lived in fear of threats from Israeli channels and military sources. After his death, Meta deleted his Instagram account, citing its policy on dangerous organisations and individuals. The account had been blocked in March 2025.",
      "IV. A toll beyond one name",
      "Aljafarawi is one of many journalists killed in Gaza since October 2023. The counts differ by who is counting.",
      "THE COUNT OF JOURNALISTS KILLED:\nCommittee to Protect Journalists (CPJ): 209 journalists and media workers killed by Israel in Gaza and in Israeli detention since 7 October 2023, as of 25 June 2026.\nCPJ is reviewing its database. It has removed eight names later established to be combatants, and 12 more for other reasons.\nGaza Government Media Office: 263 journalists killed, as stated by its director, Ismail al-Thawabta, in October 2026.\nSame office: 50 journalists detained, 23 still in prison, three missing.\nSame office: media infrastructure destroyed includes 16 satellite TV offices, 11 radio institutions, 12 newspaper offices, 23 digital media organisations and 27 printing houses, with losses estimated at about $1 billion.",
      "Whichever count is used, the scale is not in dispute. Reporting at the time of Aljafarawi’s death described the war as the deadliest conflict on record for media professionals, with more than 200 journalists killed since October 2023. Journalists are how a war is seen from outside. Each one killed leaves fewer witnesses.",
      "V. Other names",
      "On 10 August 2025 an Israeli strike on a tent used by journalists near Al-Shifa Hospital in Gaza City killed six people, according to CPJ: correspondents Anas al-Sharif and Mohammed Qreiqeh, camera operators Ibrahim Zaher and Mohammed Noufal, and freelancers Moamen Aliwa and Mohammad al-Khaldi.",
      "Israel’s military said Anas al-Sharif headed a militant cell and had been advancing rocket attacks. CPJ said Israel has a documented pattern of accusing journalists of terrorism without providing any credible proof, and that no such claim was made about the other journalists killed.",
      "“Israel is murdering the messengers.” — Sara Qudah, Regional Director, Committee to Protect Journalists",
      "VI. What the law says",
      "Under international humanitarian law, journalists are civilians. They are protected from attack unless they take a direct part in hostilities (Additional Protocol I to the Geneva Conventions, Article 79; ICRC customary rule 34). Where a journalist is accused of belonging to an armed group, the accusation is to be proved, not asserted, and an independent investigation is the way to test it.",
      "Palestinian journalists record the occupation, and the Palestinian resistance to it, for a world that has often been slow to look. Their testimony is part of the historical record, and it is gone when they are.",
      "For the families of those killed, that is the demand that remains: independent and public investigations, in every case, whoever the accused may be. In Aljafarawi’s case that means establishing who fired the shots, on whose orders, and why.",
